---
import DocSection from '../common/DocSection.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../ui/CodeBlock.astro'
import InlineCode from '../ui/InlineCode.astro'
import {
  ATOMIC_CODE,
  ATOMIC_RELATIONSHIP_CODE,
  EXTENSION_CODE
} from '../../lib/content'
---

<DocSection
  id="extensions"
  kicker="Extensions"
  title="The spec keeps going. So does Fetchja."
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    The <InlineCode>extensions</InlineCode> option registers JSON:API extensions.
    The first official one is
    <a
      class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4"
      href="https://jsonapi.org/ext/atomic/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
    >
      Atomic Operations
    </a>: an ordered, all-or-nothing batch of writes sent to a single endpoint.
  </Fragment>

  <CodeBlock code={ATOMIC_CODE} filename="atomic.ts" />

  <p class="mb-5 max-w-[44em] text-text-mut">
    The batch is posted to <InlineCode>operations</InlineCode>, and only that
    request carries the extension media type. Operations are built with
    <InlineCode>add</InlineCode>, <InlineCode>update</InlineCode>,
    <InlineCode>remove</InlineCode>, and <InlineCode>raw</InlineCode>. A
    <InlineCode>lid</InlineCode> links resources that do not exist yet.
    <InlineCode>results</InlineCode> comes back positional — one entry per
    operation, in the order you sent them.
  </p>

  <h3
    class="mt-11 mb-3.5 flex items-center gap-3 text-[21px] font-semibold
      tracking-normal text-text"
  >
    <span class="font-mono text-lg font-medium text-accent/60">#</span>
    Target a relationship
  </h3>
  <p class="mb-5 max-w-[44em] text-text-mut">
    Pass a <InlineCode>ref</InlineCode> object instead of a model name to write
    a relationship directly. <InlineCode>null</InlineCode> clears a to-one, and
    an array adds or drops to-many members.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code={ATOMIC_RELATIONSHIP_CODE} filename="relationship.ts" />

  <h3
    class="mt-11 mb-3.5 flex items-center gap-3 text-[21px] font-semibold
      tracking-normal text-text"
  >
    <span class="font-mono text-lg font-medium text-accent/60">#</span>
    Write your own
  </h3>
  <p class="mb-5 max-w-[44em] text-text-mut">
    An extension is a plain object. It installs methods on the client and hooks
    into every request, response, and error, in registration order.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code={EXTENSION_CODE} filename="extension.ts" />

  <aside
    class="mb-6 flex gap-3.5 rounded-xl border border-accent/25 bg-accent/10
      px-5 py-4"
  >
    <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-accent">
      <svg
        class="size-5"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
        stroke-width="2"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
        <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" />
        <path d="M12 16v-4M12 8h.01" />
      </svg>
    </span>
    <p class="m-0 text-[15px] text-text">
      Extension media types are never added to your everyday requests. A server
      that has never heard of the extension keeps answering the rest.
    </p>
  </aside>
</DocSection>
